                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_9.png" id="765-0" width=27 height=27 alt="9 polar patterns" title="Cardioid, Omni, Figure 8, and six intermediate positions" ></span>AKG Acoustics Perception 820 Tube</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>AKG&#8217;s Perception 820 Tube large-diaphragm, multipattern tube design with transformer-coupled output. It is the only tube microphone in AKG&#8217;s &#8220;Perception&#8221; line of project studio microphones.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/820Tube-circuit.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/820Tube-circuit-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="AKG Perception 820 Tube circuit" width="133" /></a></p><blockquote><p><cite>AKG</cite></p><p>The Perception 220, 420, 820, 100, 200 and 400 share the same capsule platform which is is one-inch, center-terminated and true condenser.</p></blockquote><p>The amplifier circuit is built using discrete, through-hole components.</p><p>The external power supply unit, dubbed the &#8220;remote control,&#8221; provides access to 9 polar patterns &#8212; from omni to cardioid (with three unnamed wide-cardioid patterns in between), and from cardioid to figure-8 (with three unnamed supercardioid patterns in between). Front-panels switches enable the high-pass filter (-12dB/octave @ 80Hz) and -20dB pad. A switch on the rear panel enables a ground lift, useful for eliminating hum from ground loops in sketchy home/studio wiring.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/perception-820-power-supply.jpg" width="200" alt="AKG Perception 820 Tube power supply" class="thumb shaft" />The tube in the amp circuit is an ECC83 (12AX7 type) dual triode of unknown (but likely Chinese) origin. The tube-and-transformer circuit is said to &#8220;emphasize even-order harmonics for rich, smooth and three-dimensional sound.&#8221;</p><p>The mic&#8217;s body is tooled from zinc/aluminum alloy. The grille is fashioned from stainless steel wire.</p><p>The 820 Tube kit contains the microphone, the &#8220;remote control&#8221; aka power supply, a 7-pin mic/power cable, an elastic shock mount, PSU power cable, and padded metal flight case.</p><p>AKG&#8217;s Perception microphones were designed by AKG in Vienna. Manufacturing is done in China, under AKG&#8217;s &#8220;strict quality control.&#8221;</p><p>The &#8220;Perception 820&#8221; branding was retired in 2014, although the same microphone was simultaneously (re)introduced as the &#8220;P820 Tube,&#8221; within AKG&#8217;s revamped collection of project studio microphones.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/Perception-820-Tube">AKG Acoustics Perception 820 Tube</a> is also known as: 820 Tube, Perception 820, 820.</p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
